    "case_id": "259",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 80s was brought to the emergency department after a cardiac arrest. According to paramedics, the patient was last seen healthy by his family 4 hours earlier, then was found unresponsive and pulseless in the bathroom. He underwent cardiopulmonary resuscitation, receiving 4 doses of epinephrine intravenously and endotracheal tube intubation with return of spontaneous circulation after approximately 10 minutes. Family later revealed he had chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and had been complaining of shortness of breath for 2 days prior to presentation. A family member witnessed him struggling for air before losing consciousness.\n\nInitial rhythm was pulseless electrical activity.\n\n- ECG findings:    - Sinus rhythm at approximately 100 beats per minute    - Right axis deviation    - Prolonged QRS duration with rsR' complexes in lead V1    - Wide S waves in leads I and V6    - Prolonged r to peak R' time in lead V1    - QT interval of 360 ms measurable in leads V4-V6    - QRS duration of 133 ms in inferior leads  - Coronary angiography:    No acute occlusions found  Image Title: Figure 1. 12-Lead Electrocardiogram at Presentation  Image Title: Figure 2. Inferior and Lateral Leads From Repeated Electrocardiogram Demonstrating Discernible T Waves  Image Title: Figure 3. Leads II, III, V5, and V6 of Initial Electrocardiogram",
